  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This open-access scholarly book is showing early uptake: OpenAlex records 5 citations, and OAPEN reports 3,112 downloads, suggesting that it has been actively read as well as cited. Its influence is concentrated in education-facing scholarship on colonialism, memory, and public space, with particularly visible reuse in work on contested symbols in Brussels.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2023, The Leopard, the Lion, and the Cock examines the long-term effects and legacies of Belgium\u2019s colonial past after Congolese independence. The record shows a book that is already entering scholarly conversation: OpenAlex counts 5 citations, which is an early but meaningful signal for a recent monograph.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature suggests that the book is resonating most strongly in research on historical studies and socio-cultural analysis, cultural identity and heritage, and public space. Three 2024 citations appear in Brussels Studies, where authors returned to themes of contested symbols in the urban landscape, indicating direct intellectual reuse in work on decolonial debate and commemoration. Additional citations in Krisis and a conference proceeding point to related interest in iconoclasm, politics, and international history.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence is stronger still. OAPEN reports 3,112 downloads, showing substantial reader engagement for a scholarly book. Because downloads capture access rather than citation, they should be read as evidence of reach and consultation rather than scholarly endorsement, but together with the citation pattern they indicate that the book is circulating in both academic and reading communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The openness of the book likely supports this reach: the record identifies it as gold open access, and OpenAIRE lists an open instance of the work. The book is also associated with Knowledge Unlatched support, reinforcing its open-access profile. Overall, the evidence points to a book with clear early scholarly uptake and strong usage, especially in research communities engaged with colonial legacies, heritage, and public memory.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the impact record remains compact. There are no event data in the digest, so the narrative of influence is grounded primarily in citations and downloads rather than in broader public or policy signals. That makes this best described as a book with visible early academic traction and substantial access, rather than one with a large multi-channel impact profile.</p>",
